        > · Published March 31, 2026 · Updated November 9, 2026
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose mb-10 border-l-2 border-primary/60 pl-5">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-primary mb-2">Quick answer</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-default dark:text-slate-200 leading-relaxed m-0">
          Critical Access Hospital scheduling is nurse rostering at federally designated rural hospitals of 25 beds or
          fewer, governed by the CMS Conditions of Participation, Texas hospital licensing rules, and the 40-hour weekly
          FLSA overtime threshold. SimpleScheduleAI is an AI-native nurse scheduling service built specifically for
          Texas Critical Access Hospitals where one nurse manager runs scheduling on top of clinical shifts, with
          limited IT to operate a self-serve platform.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What Makes Critical Access Hospital Scheduling Different?</h2>
      <p>
        Critical access hospital scheduling is the process by which Medicare-designated rural hospitals (25 or fewer
        inpatient beds) build, post, and maintain nursing rosters under <a
          href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-485/subpart-F"
          rel="noopener"
          target="_blank">CMS Conditions of Participation (42 CFR Part 485, Subpart F)</a
        >, <a href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/54-healthcare-overtime" rel="noopener" target="_blank"
          >FLSA overtime thresholds</a
        >, and Texas hospital licensing rules. It differs from large-system scheduling in one structural way: the roster
        is small enough that a single callout can leave a unit uncovered.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you have tried <a href="/nurse-scheduling-software">nurse scheduling software</a> before and walked away because
        nothing fit a 20-bed hospital, you are not alone. CAH scheduling is fundamentally different from large-system scheduling
        because the margin for error is near zero. With 25 or fewer acute care beds, a single callout can leave a unit uncovered.
        There is no float pool, no agency desk on retainer, and only limited IT to support scheduling software. The nurse
        manager handles everything, often on a personal phone at 5 AM on a Sunday.
      </p>
      <h2>How Many Critical Access Hospitals Are There in the US and Texas?</h2>
      <p>
        Critical access hospitals are federally designated rural facilities certified under <a
          href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-485/subpart-F"
          rel="noopener"
          target="_blank">CMS Conditions of Participation (42 CFR Part 485, Subpart F)</a
        >. There are approximately 1,350 CAHs in the United States, with 84 in Texas alone, serving rural communities
        where the nearest alternative hospital may be 30 to 60 miles away.
      </p>
      <p>
        The volume is small enough that spreadsheets feel manageable until they aren't. The coverage requirements are
        unforgiving: whenever there is an inpatient, a licensed nurse must be on duty, and in Texas a registered nurse
        must be on duty in every building with patients. And when a nurse calls out, the charge nurse personally works
        the phone tree until someone comes in.
      </p>

      <h2>What Are the Core Scheduling Challenges at Critical Access Hospitals?</h2>
      <p>
        Five problems repeat across nearly every CAH we interviewed: manual roster management, callout coverage gaps,
        overtime creep, fairness conflicts, and compliance documentation. Each costs you directly: lost weekend time,
        uncontrolled overtime payroll, staff burnout, and audit risk. Each worsens with spreadsheets.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Manual roster management:</strong> Most CAHs still use Excel or paper. Building a 4-week schedule takes
          4 to 6 hours and has to be rebuilt from scratch each cycle.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Callout coverage gaps:</strong> The manual phone tree for callout replacement takes 1 to 3 hours on average
          and often ends in mandatory overtime or uncovered shifts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Overtime creep:</strong> Without automated overtime tracking, managers often don't realize a nurse is approaching
          40 hours until it's too late to adjust the schedule.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fairness conflicts:</strong> When schedules are built manually, the same nurses tend to draw undesirable
          shifts, including nights, weekends, and holidays, creating morale and retention problems.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Compliance documentation:</strong> Texas hospital licensing rules and <a
            href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-485/subpart-F"
            rel="noopener"
            target="_blank">CMS Conditions of Participation</a
          > require staffing documentation that manual systems create inconsistently.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Are the Staffing Rules for a Critical Access Hospital?</h2>
      <p>
        Two rules decide who must be on duty at a critical access hospital in Texas. The federal rule sets the floor,
        Texas raises it for registered nurses, and neither one sets a nurse-to-patient ratio.
      </p>
      <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800">
            <tr>
              <th
                class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-left text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
                >Rule</th
              >
              <th
                class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-left text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
                >Source</th
              >
              <th
                class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-left text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
                >What it requires</th
              >
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-900">
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Federal floor</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                ><a
                  href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-485/subpart-F/section-485.631"
                  rel="noopener"
                  target="_blank"
                  class="text-primary underline">42 CFR 485.631(a)(5)</a
                ></td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >A registered nurse, clinical nurse specialist, or licensed practical nurse on duty whenever the
                hospital has one or more inpatients</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >What Texas adds</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                ><a
                  href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/texas/26-Tex-Admin-Code-SS-505-41"
                  rel="noopener"
                  target="_blank"
                  class="text-primary underline">26 TAC 505.41(o)(2)(D)</a
                ></td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >A registered nurse on duty in each building that has at least one nursing unit where patients are
                present</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Nurse-to-patient ratio</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                ><a
                  href="https://codes.findlaw.com/tx/health-and-safety-code/health-safety-sect-257-003"
                  rel="noopener"
                  target="_blank"
                  class="text-primary underline">Texas Health and Safety Code 257.003</a
                ></td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >No fixed ratio, federal or state. Texas requires a written nurse staffing policy and plan so that the
                number and skill mix of nurses meets the level of patient care needed</td
              >
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The difference shows up at night. An overnight shift covered only by an LPN meets the federal rule but fails the
        Texas rule, because Texas wants a registered nurse in every building with patients. Building layout and how the
        hospital is licensed affect how the Texas rule applies, so confirm the details with your compliance lead.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the full list of federal conditions, see <a href="/blog/critical-access-hospital-requirements"
          >critical access hospital requirements</a
        >. For how staffing levels get set without a ratio, see <a
          href="/blog/nurse-staffing-ratios-critical-access-hospital"
          >nurse staffing ratios at critical access hospitals</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>What Are the Texas Compliance Requirements for CAH Nurse Scheduling?</h2>
      <p>
        Texas CAHs must comply with <a
          href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-485/subpart-F"
          rel="noopener"
          target="_blank">CMS Conditions of Participation (42 CFR Part 485, Subpart F)</a
        >, Texas hospital licensing rules (administered by HHSC under 26 TAC Chapter 505), and <a
          href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/54-healthcare-overtime"
          rel="noopener"
          target="_blank">FLSA overtime rules</a
        >. The nurse coverage rules are in the section above. The rest come down to overtime pay for all hours worked
        above 40 in a workweek for non-exempt staff, pay records, and state hospital licensing.
      </p>
      <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800">
            <tr>
              <th
                class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-left text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
                >Rule</th
              >
              <th
                class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-left text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
                >Primary Source</th
              >
              <th
                class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-left text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"
                >What It Requires</th
              >
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-900">
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Overtime pay</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
                <a
                  href="https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/54-healthcare-overtime"
                  rel="noopener"
                  target="_blank"
                  class="text-primary underline">DOL FLSA Fact Sheet #54</a
                >
              </td>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >1.5× pay for hours over 40 in a workweek for non-exempt staff</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Shift premium documentation</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
                <a
                  href="https://www.twc.texas.gov/programs/wage-and-hour/texas-payday-law"
                  rel="noopener"
                  target="_blank"
                  class="text-primary underline">Texas Payday Law (TWC)</a
                >
              </td>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Written record of shift premiums and overtime calculations</td
              >
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Hospital licensing</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Texas HHSC, 26 TAC Chapter 505</td
              >
              <td class="border border-slate-300 dark:border-slate-700 px-3 py-2 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"
                >Staffing records and licensing compliance for general/special hospitals</td
              >
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Keep a record of every schedule change, callout, and replacement. Manual scheduling systems leave gaps in that
        record. SimpleScheduleAI keeps a change record of every schedule edit and sends it to you on request.
      </p>

      <h2>How Does SimpleScheduleAI Handle Critical Access Hospital Scheduling?</h2>
      <p>
        SimpleScheduleAI is an AI-native nurse scheduling service, not a software license. Upload your existing nurse
        roster via Excel. Our team configures your shift rules and compliance requirements. You receive three AI-drafted
        schedule options each cycle for your review and approval. When a nurse calls out, the system produces a ranked
        replacement shortlist from available, qualified staff in seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        You make the final call on every schedule and every replacement. The service cuts the 8 to 12 hours of weekly
        administrative work. The clinical judgment stays with you.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Not right for:</strong> Hospitals with more than 50 beds or those needing deep EHR integration. SimpleScheduleAI
        is purpose-built for the critical access context.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked Example: Staffing Math for a 25-Bed CAH on 12-Hour Shifts</h2>
      <p>
        Consider a 25-bed critical access hospital running med-surg and a small ED on two 12-hour shifts. To keep a
        registered nurse on duty whenever patients are in the building, as Texas requires, and hold safe staffing
        levels, the unit needs roughly 4 to 6 nurses on days and 3 to 5 on nights, every day of the year. That is 7 to
        11 nurse shifts a day, or roughly 50 to 75 shifts a week before a single callout.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-default dark:text-slate-100 mb-3">
          Sample 7-Day Nurse Schedule: 25-Bed CAH, June 2-8, 2026
        </h3>
